import{_ as t,o as r,c as o,a0 as a}from"./chunks/framework.D4fUSYzX.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"ADR 0143: On the herdr-core host the plane owns the worktree lifecycle","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0143-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-owns-the-worktree-lifecycle.md","filePath":"adr/0143-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-owns-the-worktree-lifecycle.md"}'),n={name:"adr/0143-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-owns-the-worktree-lifecycle.md"};function s(h,e,d,c,i,l){return r(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="adr-0143-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-owns-the-worktree-lifecycle" tabindex="-1">ADR 0143: On the herdr-core host the plane owns the worktree lifecycle <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0143-on-the-herdr-core-host-the-plane-owns-the-worktree-lifecycle" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ADR 0143: On the herdr-core host the plane owns the worktree lifecycle&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Status: accepted Date: 2026-11-12</p><p>Records that a Worktree Environment on the herdr-core host is a git worktree the plane creates and removes itself, in herdr&#39;s own worktrees directory and by herdr&#39;s own naming rule, so one Ticket keeps one checkout across a host switch, and that the reopen lookup reads git&#39;s record rather than one host&#39;s list. It changes no worktree rule the operator reads, decides no gate, and lands no code (<a href="./0141-the-plane-resolves-its-agent-host-herdr-or-herdr-core.html">ADR 0141</a>, <a href="./0142-one-run-holds-agents-on-both-hosts-and-the-host-stands-on-the-handoff.html">ADR 0142</a>).</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On the herdr host the worktree is herdr&#39;s. The plane asks for <code>worktree create</code>, reads <code>worktree list --cwd</code>, and removes it with <code>worktree remove --workspace</code>, which takes herdr&#39;s workspace down with the checkout. The rules built on that are the plane&#39;s own and are load-bearing: the reopen by branch, the reopen by path when git no longer records the worktree (<code>findTicketWorktreePath</code>, which takes the parent of the repository&#39;s linked worktrees and the branch&#39;s name), the Leftover worktree directory the plane moves aside instead of deleting (ADR 0062), the residue record that removes only what one start created (issue #204), and the merge ask&#39;s Close that takes the checkout down with its workspace and keeps the branch (ADR 0124).</p><p>herdr-core answers no worktree command at all: its unit is a terminal with a name, a cwd, and labels (<a href="./0141-the-plane-resolves-its-agent-host-herdr-or-herdr-core.html">ADR 0141</a>). Two measured facts say the host is not the only keeper of the truth. On 2026-11-12, against this repository:</p><ul><li>herdr&#39;s layout is <code>&lt;home&gt;/.herdr/worktrees/&lt;repo name&gt;/&lt;branch with each slash for a hyphen&gt;</code>. Every worktree the plane has is there, named from its branch (<code>factory-consultation-ba35ab04-grill-with-docs</code> for <code>factory/consultation-ba35ab04-grill-with-docs</code>).</li><li><code>git worktree list</code> on the source checkout already holds every worktree herdr created, at those same paths and on those same branches, and herdr&#39;s own <code>worktree list</code> answer is git&#39;s shape (branch, path, <code>is_linked_worktree</code>, <code>is_prunable</code>) with herdr&#39;s workspace facts beside it.</li></ul><p>So a worktree the plane makes with git is findable by the same reads that find herdr&#39;s, and the plane already runs git for the Worktree base&#39;s default-branch rule (ADR 0075).</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>The Environment kinds stand on both hosts.</strong> The launcher and the override panel offer live checkout and worktree whichever host the start takes, and a host never answers one of the plane&#39;s two Environments with a refusal.</p><p><strong>On the herdr-core host the plane runs git directly</strong> for the worktree a start creates, the branch it creates with it, and the removal its residue record and the operator&#39;s Close ask for. The plane still never starts the Agent process itself (ADR 0002): it makes the checkout, then asks herdr-core to run the Agent in it.</p><p><strong>The path and the name follow herdr&#39;s rule.</strong> The checkout stands under <code>&lt;home&gt;/.herdr/worktrees/&lt;repo name&gt;/</code>, named from the branch with each slash for a hyphen, so a Ticket whose first Handoff ran on herdr and whose next runs on herdr-core reopens the same checkout on the same branch instead of forking a second working copy of its own work. The plane owns no second worktrees directory.</p><p><strong>The reopen lookup reads git, and one lookup serves both hosts.</strong> The branch lookup and the leftover-path lookup of <code>readWorktreePaths</code> answer from git&#39;s own record of the checkout&#39;s linked worktrees, because git holds both hosts&#39; worktrees, and herdr&#39;s extra fact (<code>open_workspace_id</code>) is read only where a herdr workspace has to be taken down with the checkout.</p><p><strong>Each host&#39;s cleanup stays on its own side of the boundary.</strong> On herdr, <code>worktree remove --workspace</code> and the workspace and tab closes stand as they are. On herdr-core, the residue record removes the terminal with <code>hc kill</code> and <code>hc rm</code> and the checkout with git, and Close takes the checkout down with its terminal. The plane runs no <code>git worktree remove</code> against a checkout a herdr workspace still holds: that cleanup is herdr&#39;s to run, because only herdr&#39;s <code>worktree remove</code> clears herdr&#39;s row, and an unconfirmed close leaves its resource standing in the record exactly as it does today.</p><p><strong>The Leftover worktree directory rule is the plane&#39;s on both hosts.</strong> The move aside under a name that says what it is, and the never-delete rule (ADR 0062), run wherever the blocking directory stands, since the directory is a fact of the branch&#39;s name and not of a host.</p><h2 id="considered-options" tabindex="-1">Considered options <a class="header-anchor" href="#considered-options" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Considered options&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Refuse the worktree Environment on herdr-core.</strong> Rejected, and it was the first candidate: a half host that runs only one of the plane&#39;s two Environments forks every branch of the Handoff&#39;s environment builder on a host test, which is the shape that turns into permanent special cases.</li><li><strong>Give the herdr-core host its own plane-owned worktrees directory.</strong> Rejected: a Ticket that changes host mid-cycle gets a second checkout with a second copy of its own branch state, and the reopen-by-path rule then has two directories to guess between.</li><li><strong>Let the Agent create its own worktree.</strong> Rejected: the branch, the base, the placement write, and the residue record are the plane&#39;s facts before the Agent starts, and a start that cannot state its own checkout cannot roll back what it made.</li><li><strong>Keep reading herdr&#39;s <code>worktree list</code> for the reopen lookup even on the herdr-core host.</strong> Rejected: it puts a herdr dependency on a run whose host may be herdr-core alone, and it answers nothing about a checkout the plane made itself while git already answers about both.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The plane owns the worktree path naming rule on both hosts, and that rule is a contract with herdr&#39;s directory layout. A herdr that changes where or how it names its worktrees is a measured break the plane&#39;s own lookup has to follow; the layout above is recorded against herdr 0.9.5.</li><li>The Worktree Environment&#39;s residue kinds stop naming host objects one for one: on herdr-core a start&#39;s residue is one terminal and one checkout, and the record&#39;s confirmation rows read that way rather than pretending to a tab and a workspace.</li><li>A host switch inside one Ticket cycle keeps one checkout, one branch, and one pull request, which is the point of taking herdr&#39;s path rule.</li><li>git becomes an actor in the Handoff&#39;s environment builder on one host, so the git refusal shapes that ADR 0132&#39;s world already answers (a path that cannot be checked out, a stale record, a prunable worktree) are in the Handoff&#39;s path on that host and are answered there, not only in the Worktree base.</li><li>The verification record states which worktree flows ran on herdr-core and which did not; no claim of parity is extended past the flows that ran, and the screen-reader path stays unverified as before.</li></ul>',19)])])}const k=t(n,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,k as default};
